text:
Research on Royal Australian Air Force (RAAF) pilot training units during World War II has identified several Elementary Flying Training Schools (EFTS). The units include: 
- No. 8 EFTS, operating from September 1940 to December 1944 at Narrandera, New South Wales; 
- No. 5 EFTS, active from June 1940 to June 1944 at Narromine, New South Wales; 
- No. 12 EFTS, initially located at Bundaberg Airport and later at Lowood Airfield, from October 1941 to April 1942; 
- No. 1 EFTS, which relocated from Melbourne, Victoria, to Parafield, South Australia, and finally to Tamworth, New South Wales, between November 1939 and December 1944; 
- No. 2 EFTS, based at Archerfield, Queensland, from November 1939 to March 1942; 
- No. 4 EFTS, operating at Mascot, New South Wales, from January 1940 to April 1942; 
- No. 3 EFTS, situated at Essendon, Victoria, from January 1940 to May 1942; 
- No. 11 EFTS, at Benalla, Victoria, from June 1941 to July 1945. 
Additionally, there is a mention of No. 1 Basic Flying Training School (No. 1 BFTS), commencing operations in December 1951, although its connection to World War II pilot training is unclear. The location of No. 3 EFTS, Essendon Airport, has a historical background dating to the early 20th century. These EFTS units played a role in the RAAF's pilot training program during World War II, with documented locations and operational dates.
